speechtext:
I will tell you this: I sometimes doubt where I am on occasions, but I am always on my feet. I never lose my balance.
We in this group believe-and I think I might as well lay the C.C.F. program in general before the house again, because a good

Disparity of National Income to Farmers thing can bear repeating-that a number of important steps must be taken before the present unsatisfactory position of agriculture can be corrected. I am quoting now, from the pamphlet which we issued during the last election containing our program for agriculture. We propose:
Guaranteed forward prices, based on parity, with the use of deficiency payments where appropriate, as a means of providing parity of income to agricultural producers;
National marketing boards, where requested by producers, for all farm products entering into inter-provincial and export trade;
Comprehensive crop insurance;
A national program to provide farmers with scientific and technical assistance;
A comprehensive land conservation and utilization program;
Removal of monopoly control of the farm machinery, fertilizer, chemical, meat-packing, flourmilling, and other industries in order to lower production costs and reduce price spreads between producers and consumers.
And then:
The C.C.F. believes that in a hungry world it is essential that food production be maintained at a high level and that the producers be assured of security and a reasonable return for their work. Vigorous government action is needed to produce this.
